    "## 6. Feature Extraction Using TF-IDF\n",
    "\n",
    "Machine learning algorithms require numerical input. Since the dataset contains text messages, the text must be converted into numerical features.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Term Frequency–Inverse Document Frequency (TF-IDF)** is a technique that assigns weights to words based on their importance within a message and across the document collection.\n",
    "\n",
    "* **Term Frequency (TF):** Measures how frequently a term occurs in a document.\n",
    "* **Inverse Document Frequency (IDF):** Reduces the weight of terms that appear in many documents.\n",
    "* **TF-IDF:** Combines these measures to represent the relative importance of terms.\n",
    "\n",
    "Common words that appear throughout many messages generally receive lower IDF weights, while terms that are more distinctive to particular messages may receive higher weights.\n",
    "\n",
    "The `TfidfVectorizer` from scikit-learn converts the cleaned messages into numerical feature vectors. The vectorizer is fitted only on the training data to prevent data leakage.\n",
    "## 7. Splitting the Dataset\n",
    "\n",
    "The dataset is divided into training and testing sets using an 80:20 ratio.\n",
    "\n",
    "* **Training set (80%):** Used to train the machine learning models.\n",
    "* **Testing set (20%):** Used to evaluate their performance on unseen messages.\n",
    "\n",
    "A fixed random state ensures reproducibility. Stratified splitting maintains approximately the same proportion of spam and ham messages in both sets.\n",
    "\n"
   ]
  },
